# SYNOPSIS

```usage
sort [-Cbcdfimnru] [-o outfile] [-t delim] [-k def]... [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`sort` reads every line of each <file>, or of standard input with none, and writes them all, sorted, on standard output; a <file> of `-` is standard input.
Lines are compared by their bytes, unless a flag says otherwise; there is no locale.
The whole input is held in memory.

: `-b`
  Ignore blanks at the start and end of each key field; it applies to the keys given after it by `-k`.
: `-d`
  Compare only blanks and letters and digits.
: `-f`
  Compare lower-case letters as upper-case.
: `-i`
  Ignore characters that are not printable.
: `-n`
  Compare the start of each key as a number, as C's `strtod` reads it.
: `-r`
  Reverse the order.
: `-k` <def>
  Sort by a key, <def> written as `field[.char][flags][,field[.char][flags]]`: the key starts at character <char>, 1 by default, of field <field>, and ends at the second position, or at the end of the line.
  Fields count from 1, and are separated by runs of blanks, or by <delim> with `-t`; <flags> are any of `b`, `d`, `f`, `i`, `n` and `r`, for that key alone.
  Several `-k` give keys compared in turn; lines equal by every key are compared whole.
: `-t` <delim>
  Separate fields by <delim>, which may be more than one character and may hold escapes such as `\t`.
: `-u`
  Write only the first of lines that compare equal; with `-c` or `-C`, equal lines are out of order.
: `-o` <outfile>
  Write to <outfile>, which may be one of the inputs, instead of standard output.
: `-c`
  Check instead of sorting: report the first line out of order and exit 1, or exit 0 if there is none.
: `-C`
  As `-c`, but report nothing.
: `-m`
  Accepted: the inputs are sorted together whole, which merges them too.

`sort` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/sort`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/sort`.
There is no native `sort`.

# EXAMPLES

As `tests/user/rctest.rc` runs it, in rc in the POSIX namespace:

```text
% printf 'c\nb\na\n' | sort
a
b
c
```

